WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.600
The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn&nbsp;
by American author Mark Twain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.280
is both considered one of the great&nbsp;
American novels and one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.560
frequently banned and contested novels due to&nbsp;
its use of the N-word and racial stereotypes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.800
This has launched many debates as to if&nbsp;
the work should even be taught in schools.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:22.001
And the larger questions of is Hucklberry Finn racist.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.000
Today we are going to attempt to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:32.240
crack the case: is Huckleberry Finn an&nbsp;
anti-racist work? Or is it just plain ol’ racist?

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:39.260
Or, as in most things, is it a little more nuanced and complex. Let's get into it. 

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.440
The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn&nbsp;
is the second book in the Tom Sawyer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.080
literary universe taking place&nbsp;
after The Adventures of Tom Sawyer.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.720
Considered one of the earliest novels to be written in&nbsp;
dialect, the story brings us into the brutal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:01.680
world of Huck Finn, a young kid who finds out&nbsp;
that his father Pap Finn, has returned to town.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:09.440
Pap Finn is a rolling stone and has a history&nbsp;
of violence and gambling, and Huck is worried that his return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:19.680
means more abuse both physically and financially. Pap tells Huck to quit&nbsp;
school and warns him not to put on an air of superiority with all of that book learning.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.640
“I’ll take you down a peg before I get done&nbsp;
with you. You’re educated, too, they say—can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.080
read and write. You think you’re&nbsp;
better’n your father, now, don’t you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.960
because he can’t? I’ll take it out of you.&nbsp;
Who told you you might meddle with such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.560
hifalut’n foolishness,&nbsp;
hey?—who told you you could?”

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:42.320
Huck continues to attend school just&nbsp;
to spite his father with knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.000
but Pap kidnaps him and takes him to a small&nbsp;
cabin on the Illinois shore. There he is beaten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.720
constantly, and so Huck fakes his own murder&nbsp;
and escapes down the Mississippi River.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.520
Huck Finn, the first Gone Girl.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:58.000
During his escape he stumbles across Jim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.680
a runaway slave who left his “master” in&nbsp;
order to avoid being sold “down the river.”

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.880
“Being sold down river” is a reference to&nbsp;
when slaves would be taken to Louisville&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.240
to be sold to cotton plantations in states&nbsp;
further south, via the Mississippi River.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.720
In his 2010 history of the Mississippi River,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.720
Wicked River: The Mississippi When It Last&nbsp;
Ran Wild journalist Lee Sandlin said "the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:26.960
threat of being 'sold down the river' was&nbsp;
seen as tantamount to a death sentence."

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.160
Both Huck and Jim are now on an adventure&nbsp;
for freedom that ends with Jim being freed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.120
because his former master released him in&nbsp;
her will. Huck Finn ends the novel saying:

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.640
“[... ]so there ain’t nothing more to write about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.000
and I am rotten glad of it, because&nbsp;
if I’d a knowed what a trouble it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.966
to make a book I wouldn’t a tackled&nbsp;
it, and ain’t a-going to no more.” Honestly related because writing a book is hard. 

00:02:50.720 --> 00:03:01.360
Samuel Langhorne Clemens aka Mark Twain (ha see&nbsp;
that, call back?) was a Missouri boy raised in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.640
Hannibal, Missouri. When his father passed away&nbsp;
at eleven, Twain became a printer's apprentice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.440
and eventually upgraded to a Printer himself.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:13.120
Despite all of this work in&nbsp;
printing, his dream at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.960
was to be a steamboatman and eventually got a&nbsp;
chance to work on a boat before the Civil War.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.400
Speaking of the Civil War, Twain served&nbsp;
in the Confederate army but then quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.360
left and saw no combat. This&nbsp;
will become relevant later.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.120
Eventually, Twain became a writer and&nbsp;
publisher, being responsible for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:38.160
publication of Ulysses S. Grant’s memoirs,&nbsp;
as well as eight novels of his own, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.120
four other novels in the Tom Sawyer extended&nbsp;
universe (with several unfinished fragments),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.000
dozens of short stories and essays,&nbsp;
as well as his own auto-biography.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.991
Because who knows you better than you?

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:54.000
Politically, Twain got a lot&nbsp;
more radical the older he got.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.160
It is noted that before 1899,&nbsp;
Twain was an imperialist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.120
but that changed following&nbsp;
the Philippine–American War,:

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.240
“I wanted the American eagle to go screaming into&nbsp;
the Pacific ... Why not spread its wings over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.520
Philippines, I asked myself? ... I said to myself,&nbsp;
Here are a people who have suffered for three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:19.840
centuries. We can make them as free as ourselves,&nbsp;
give them a government and country of their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:24.000
put a miniature of the American&nbsp;
Constitution afloat in the Pacific,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:30.400
start a brand new republic to take its&nbsp;
place among the free nations of the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:34.080
It seemed to me a great task to&nbsp;
which we had addressed ourselves.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.640
But I have thought some more, since then,&nbsp;
and I have read carefully the treaty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.000
Paris (which ended the Spanish–American War),&nbsp;
and I have seen that we do not intend to free,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.160
but to subjugate the people of the Philippines.&nbsp;
We have gone there to conquer, not to redeem.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:55.840
Despite having been enlisted in the&nbsp;
Civil War on the side of the Confederacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:59.520
Twain was against slavery but was&nbsp;
notably not great about American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.280
Indian rights in his early writings. But&nbsp;
one of the things about him having written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.480
so so so much is that we are able to see&nbsp;
his thoughts fully evolve with the times.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.360
All of this informs the way we read Huck Finn.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.960
Huck Finn is probably, along with Tom Sawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:22.880
Twain’s most well-known and cited book&nbsp;
because of the controversy surrounding it.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:28.240
The character of Jim is a slave,&nbsp;
written in a very stereotypical way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:35.360
and the “N” word is used a lot in the text and&nbsp;
he is referred to as N-word Jim in the book.

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:40.480
This has led educators and others to attempt&nbsp;
to either censor the book or ban it outright.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:46.320
It has also sparked questions as to&nbsp;
whether the book is racist or not.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:54.234
Let’s see what academia has to say about this very, very, very notable topic. 

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.720
A lot of academics take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.640
issue with Jim’s characterization in&nbsp;
the last few chapters of the book.

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:05.120
Professor Forrest G. Robinson, at the&nbsp;
University of California at Santa Cruz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:06.480
makes this observation:

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:10.880
“[...] more recent critics have been strongly&nbsp;
inclined to contrast the submissive slave who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.760
appears in the closing chapters with the more&nbsp;
complete human being who moves through the central&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:21.600
sections of the narrative. Modern observers&nbsp;
are in broad agreement that this simpler, more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.960
passive Jim is radically out of character. [...]&nbsp;
More recently, Joseph Sawicki has observed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.760
the Jim of the final chapters is&nbsp;
"reduced to a stock character."”

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.040
For clarity towards the end of the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.880
Jim is taken by plantation owners Silas and&nbsp;
Sally Phelps. Their nephew is Tom Sawyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.840
so Huck decides to pretend to be Tom&nbsp;
Sawyer in order to help Jim escape.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.280
That's part one. 

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:54.640
Then, in part two , the real Tom shows up and&nbsp;then pretends to be his own older brother in order to help the ruse.  

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:01.840
Long story short Tom comes up with a really&nbsp;
convoluted plan and ends up getting shot.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:09.040
Jim, rather than escaping, decides to stay&nbsp;
by Tom’s side giving up a chance for freedom.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.880
Professor of English Neil Schmitz points&nbsp;
out that, “ Jim absurdly decides to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.360
sacrifice his life (and the family he&nbsp;
professes to love) for Tom's sake.”

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:27.973
What makes this worse, so much worse, is that Tom knows&nbsp;
that Jim has been freed from slavery this whole time,

00:07:27.973 --> 00:07:30.960
the whole time and rather than say anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:36.880
he comes up with this ridiculous plan&nbsp;
because it’s more fun and he’s a dumb teenage boy.

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:42.000
And so the stakes are very low for him, even though they are the highest for Jim. 

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:49.360
When that is revealed, Schmitz notes of Jim,&nbsp;
“Denied the privilege of wrath, even resentment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:52.080
Jim hardens into a piece of statuary.”

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:57.760
Yet despite this writing for Jim, something&nbsp;
that is acknowledged to be limited at times, 

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:03.360
there are Black Academics who very much&nbsp;
see Huck Finn as an anti-racist text.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.200
David Smith, Professor of English at&nbsp;
Williams College, said this of Finn:

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:11.840
“It is therefore ironic that Huckleberry&nbsp;
Finn has often been attacked and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:17.360
censored as a racist work. I would argue, on&nbsp;
the contrary, that except for Melville's work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:22.560
Huckleberry Finn is without peers among&nbsp;
major Euro-American novels for its explicitly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:28.480
anti-racist stance. Those who brand the book&nbsp;
"racist"generally do so without having considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:34.080
the specific form of racial discourse to which&nbsp;
the novel responds. Furthermore, Huckleberry Finn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.840
offers much more than the typical liberal defenses&nbsp;
of "human dignity" and protests against cruelty.”

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:46.080
This is not a universal take, as&nbsp;
Dr. Bernard W. Bell makes the point about Twain:

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:49.840
“Born and bred in the antebellum Southwest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:54.960
a volunteer in the Confederate militia, and an&nbsp;
advocate of the delightful accuracy of minstrelsy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:02.000
Twain, as we will see, struggles valiantly, like&nbsp;
Huck, to reject the legacy of American racism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:07.280
and to accept his personal share of responsibility&nbsp;
for the injustice of slavery, but never in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:12.800
Adventures of Huckleberry Finn does he fully and&nbsp;
unequivocally accept the equality of blacks."

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:20.640
So, good intent but more work would could have been done in the book to make the politics clear. 

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.120
In reading Huck Finn, the writing for&nbsp;
Jim reminded me of the equally jarring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.680
depiction of slaves in Uncle Tom’s&nbsp;
Cabin by Harriet Beecher Stowe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:35.840
another book that is anti-racist in it’s intention&nbsp;
but still feels more minstrel than anti-racist in context, 

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:39.303
especially in a modern-day era. 

00:09:39.303 --> 00:09:45.200
Depicting a character like Jim in an infantilizing&nbsp;
way, the need to strip his humanity to let a white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:52.320
boy get the best of him is, in a way, comforting&nbsp;to white readers at the time. Let’s not forget Finn came out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.838
twenty years after the Civil War ended. So&nbsp;
that was still fresh in white reader’s minds.

00:09:57.838 --> 00:10:05.622
Even more so than today, when they still somehow forget that they lost, but...whatever. 

00:10:05.622 --> 00:10:10.720
It was a very big deal and even this much depiction of an enslaved person being freed might have still been seen as radical. 

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:15.280
Ultimately these choices are made so that white&nbsp;
people can feel empathy for the realities of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:21.520
enslaved people. This is why the book is so hard&nbsp;
for Black readers, especially in a school setting.

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:25.760
The late author Toni Morrison wrote&nbsp;
the introduction to The Adventures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:30.160
of Huckleberry Finn, for the Oxford&nbsp;
University Press edition. In it she says:

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:34.640
“The source of my unease reading this&nbsp;
amazing, troubling book now seems clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.365
an imperfect coming to terms with&nbsp;
three matters Twain addresses--Huck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.365 --> 00:10:46.400
Finn's estrangement, soleness and morbidity as&nbsp;
an outcast child; the disproportionate sadness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:52.560
at the center of Jim's and his relationship;&nbsp;
and the secrecy in which Huck's engagement with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:58.320
(rather than escape from) a racist society is&nbsp;
necessarily conducted. It is also clear that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:03.680
rewards of my effort to come to terms have been&nbsp;
abundant. [...] It has been extremely worthwhile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:09.520
slogging through Jim's shame and humiliation to&nbsp;
recognize the sadness, the tragic implications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:14.320
at the center of his relationship with&nbsp;
Huck. My fury at the maze of deceit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:21.600
the risk of personal harm that a white child is&nbsp;
forced to negotiate in a race-inflected society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:27.120
is dissipated by the exquisite uses to&nbsp;
which Twain puts that maze, that risk.”

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.640
The book is traumatic, but valuable something&nbsp;
that students can take something out of. Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:37.280
I feel Mark Franek and Dr. Nyaka NiiLampti sum&nbsp;
up the issue aptly in the following statement:

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:41.280
“We think that Huck Finn is an important&nbsp;
work of art that should be available in every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:45.600
library in the world, but we don't think&nbsp;
that it should be required reading in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.480
predominantly white high school where African&nbsp;
American students are in a small minority.”

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.000
In a way you risk marginalizing them even more to have all of these white classmates 

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.679
be asked and required to show

00:11:58.679 --> 00:12:05.680
a level of racial maturity when discussing the N-word. We&nbsp;
can’t even get Congress to acknowledge racism. 

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:11.813
in this county, and then we're going to ask teenagers to do it without give them the necessary tools to do so?

00:12:11.813 --> 00:12:15.680
Reading Huck Finn is to actively&nbsp;
teach the history of racism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:23.040
And that requires putting in a lot of work that&nbsp;
puts the book in conversation with other works, and a time period. 

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:29.253
Not simply rushing through it&nbsp;
because it shows up on the SATs or G.R.E. as a required text in American reading. 

00:12:29.253 --> 00:12:31.735
It is an education experience. 

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:36.560
I don’t believe in banning books, but I believe&nbsp;
that if we are going to teach Huck Finn then that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:42.160
means teaching the America that Mark Twain was&nbsp;
writing about and the audience he was writing for.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:46.880
I want to end with these words by&nbsp;
Associate Professor Ebony Elizabeth Thomas:

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:47.091
“[....] When chattel slavery is focalized&nbsp;
through the perspective of Huck instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.091 --> 00:12:47.280
of Jim, we run the risk of allowing a&nbsp;
single story to shape our classrooms.

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:52.080
When we teach Adventures of Huckleberry Finn,&nbsp;
To Kill a Mockingbird, and other classics of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:56.960
American literature, do we provide room&nbsp;
for contemporary voices? What would it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:01.840
mean to pair Narrative of the Life of Frederick&nbsp;
Douglass with selected excerpts from Huck Finn?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:08.160
How about teaching the Scottsboro trial and&nbsp;
screening speeches from Civil Rights-era thinkers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:12.800
during our To Kill a Mockingbird literature units&nbsp;
(Thomas, Reese, and Lesesne). Or is it time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:18.720
revisit the canon completely, rethinking how we&nbsp;
tacitly narrate our national story through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:24.560
literature that we teach? Our collective answers&nbsp;
to these questions have significance not only for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:33.840
the contemporary moment but also for English&nbsp;
teaching and learning in the decades to come.”

